    {
      "game": "Nioh 3",
      "game_image": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/products/19484/450x258/nioh-3-pc-steam-cover.jpg?v=1793275200",
      "game_url": "https://www.instant-gaming.com/en/19484-buy-nioh-3-pc-steam/",
      "user": "Imran",
      "avatar": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/avatars/31874599-1783652626.jpg",
      "sentiment": "like",
      "content": "(For PS5 / PC)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nTeam Ninja returns with the sharpest entry yet in the Nioh series — blending its signature punishing, skill‑heavy combat with bigger, more open historical battlefields . Taking the role of Tokugawa Takechiyo, you journey through war‑torn eras, hunting twisted yokai demons while learning to master two distinct fighting styles: methodical, heavy‑hitting Samurai Style, and fast, agile Ninja Style — swap between them mid‑fight to outmanoeuvre every foe.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe combat is the clear star here. Timed deflects, Ki Pulse management, and a huge range of weapons and build options make every battle feel tense and deeply satisfying — once you get the rhythm down, landing perfect combos against towering bosses is incredibly rewarding . The move toward large open zones gives more freedom than past games; you can tackle objectives in your own order, hunt optional yokai, or team up with friends in 3‑player co‑op when things get too tough . It’s also the most accessible Nioh yet — quality‑of‑life tweaks and clearer learning curves help new players find their footing, even if it never goes easy on you .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThat said, it isn’t perfect. Some areas suffer from repeated enemy layouts, a few boss fights have brutal difficulty spikes, and frame‑rate stutters can pop up during busy battles . The story is serviceable but fairly straightforward — mostly a backdrop for the action rather than a deep narrative standout .\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nIf you loved Nioh 1 & 2, this is an easy must‑buy — it builds on everything that made the series great while expanding its scope. For soulslike‑curious newcomers, it’s a solid place to start — just be ready to learn from every defeat.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nFinal score: 8/10 — A sharp, polished samurai‑action thrill ride with plenty of depth for dedicated players"
    },
    {
      "game": "Bus Simulator 27",
      "game_image": "https://gaming-cdn.com/images/products/19771/450x258/bus-simulator-27-pc-steam-cover.jpg?v=1793275200",
      "game_url": "https://www.instant-gaming.com/en/19771-buy-bus-simulator-27-pc-steam/",
      "user": "gamer-830e6f",
      "avatar": "https://gaming-cdn.com/themes/igv2/images/avatar2.svg",
      "sentiment": "like",
      "content": "I’ve owned both *Bus Simulator 18* and *Bus Simulator 21*, and to be completely honest, they were okay, but far from perfect\\. In fact, the last time I played *Bus Simulator 21: Next Stop*, I encountered so many crashes and bugs that I ended up closing the game and haven’t touched it since\\.\n\n\n\nBecause of my previous experiences, I was expecting this game to follow the same pattern, with a Season Pass and the same old issues\\. However, SimTrac seems to have taken a different approach, and I have to say, I’m genuinely impressed\\!\n\n\n\nThe game worked straight away with my Logitech G27 wheel, without me having to map all the controls myself\\. That’s always a welcome feature and made getting started so much easier\\.\n\n\n\nAnother thing I’m really enjoying is the story aspect of the game\\. It’s something different, and it makes the experience more enjoyable for me\\. I genuinely hope SimTrac continues in this direction and that future games are just as enjoyable as this one\\.\n\n\n\nAt the time of writing, the game is on sale on Steam with 20% off, bringing the price down to £19\\.99\\. If you’re on the fence about buying it, I’d highly recommend giving it a go\\!\n\n\n\nOverall, I’m pleasantly surprised by this game, and I’m looking forward to seeing what SimTrac does next\\."
    }
